# The reconciler of a repository's charters, for the repository `{{.Repo}}` (git-issue dzxn44gw;
# the design is j5vbwvja and its parts, and the decision on j5vbwvja: the gate is the install,
# not the merge). RENDERED by system/repocharters from charter.tony.tmpl: edit the template.
#
# WHAT IT KEEPS TRUE: what runs in verse is the last state of the repository's charters directory
# on main that an ask answered yes to, however it reached main. These records keep the states
# apart, and every rule's subject is one of them or an effect of one:
#
#   repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.state.main           what is on main: {sha, tree}
#   repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.state.live           what is on verse/live: {sha, tree}
#   repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.candidate.<tree>     one per charters tree ever on main or proposed by
#                                                a pull request to main: {tree, ask, title, seen},
#                                                `sha` where main carries it, `checked` once
#                                                checked, then `answer` and `askedAt`
#   repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.verdict.<tree>       what verse says about the tree on a pull request
#                                                that proposes it: {decision, check, text}
#   repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.pull.<number>        what an open pull request to main proposes now:
#                                                {pr, head, tree}, `tree` empty for nothing
#   repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.livecheck.live       the minute's check of verse/live against what is
#                                                installed, and the git io's answer beside it
#   repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.running              verse's note of its last install: {sha, tree}
#
# A tree is the sha of the charters directory's tree object, as the git io reflects it with
# `-tree <path>` (`tree.{{.Tree}}` on every ref): the identity of the directory's contents, the
# same across commits that differ elsewhere and after a squash. The answer stands for the tree, so
# a change answered yes once installs on every commit that carries it, a rollback included, with
# no second ask.
#
# THE EMPTY TREE ({{.EmptyTree}}) is the directory with nothing in it, or gone: a state like any
# other. Its question asks to remove every charter at `{{.InstallAt}}`, and installing it does.
#
# THE ASK is a task's: the propose rule spawns a call list whose one call is `ask`, so the question
# is on the docket as any ask is, answerable from the CLI, Slack, or the pull request below, and
# its answer is the task's result, written onto the candidate. Its id is the task's with its first
# call's number, `repo-charters.{{.Repo}}:propose@repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.candidate.<tree>~1`, which is how
# the pull request names it.
#
# THE ANSWER is read as text, `<principal> chose <option>` with `: <words>` after it: the option is
# the word after the principal, so the words never read as a choice (system/repocharters,
# answeredProceed).
#
# ON THE PULL REQUEST TO MAIN, first: a pull request to main that changes the charters (the github
# io's `-tree`, against the head's merge base) proposes the tree at its head, and the question is
# asked there, where the change is reviewed. Verse requests changes on it until the question is
# answered yes AND its charter check finds the tree installs, which holds the merge where main's
# protection requires approving reviews, and posts its `verse` status (the answer) and its
# `verse/check` status (the check) on the head. What it says follows the tree the pull request
# proposes NOW: pushed on to another tree, it is asked about that one in the same comment; once it
# leaves the charters alone, the request for changes is dismissed and the comment says so. The
# answer stands for the tree, so when the pull request merges, main carries a tree already answered
# and it installs with no second question.
#
# THE verse/live PULL REQUEST is for a change that reached main without that answer (an ordinary
# push, a pull request merged around verse): the question is rendered where a reviewer already is,
# a branch at the candidate's commit, opened onto verse/live, so its diff is what is on main
# against what runs, with the question as its comment, the answer heard there, and verse's commit
# status on its head saying what verse waits for and then what it decided. It is opened only once
# verse/live stands, while the candidate is main's and not what verse/live holds, and closed as
# superseded when main moves on; its question stands either way. A no closes it with the commit
# status failed.
#
# THE CHECK is apart from the decision and from the install: each candidate tree is checked against
# what runs by the verse's charter check, which installs nothing, and its verdict is a status of
# its own on either pull request (`verse/check`); while main's tree would be refused an issue says
# so, and it closes when main's tree passes.
#
# THE INSTALL FOLLOWS verse/live, which is what runs: the git io reflects verse/live's charters
# tree as it reflects main's, and a tree on verse/live whose candidate was answered yes is
# installed, by the git io, from its own copy of the repository, at `{{.InstallAt}}`, as itself,
# pruning what the tree leaves out (iod reference, the out half of git). So a commit to main that
# leaves the charters alone changes nothing the install reads, and installs nothing. A yes on
# main's tree moves verse/live to the candidate's commit (the git io, as the App): made there at
# the first install, moved on a later yes or a rollback; a candidate pull request whose head is
# that commit is then merged by GitHub's own reckoning, its head having reached its base. The
# io's status says what it installed and for which commit, and the rules read that: what runs is
# recorded, the tree is tagged once, and the pull request's commit status passes. An install the
# io refuses is a GitHub issue, closed by the next install that succeeds, and the pull request's
# commit status fails. A tree on verse/live nobody answered yes to is not installed.
#
# NOTHING STANDS UNSAID. Each way main, verse/live and the installed charters can disagree has an
# outcome somebody sees:
#
#   main's tree unanswered          its question, on the pull request onto verse/live
#   main's tree answered no         an issue, open while main holds it
#   main's check refused            an issue, open until main's tree passes
#   an install refused              an issue, open until an install succeeds
#   verse/live not what runs        an issue, open until verse/live is back in step: the installed
#                                   charters are checked against verse/live's tree every minute
#                                   (the reconciler's own schedule), and two rounds differing at
#                                   one commit open it, so an install under way is not taken for
#                                   it. Neither restored nor asked about at the time: the ask comes
#                                   with a pull request to fix it. A verse/live that is gone is
#                                   the same issue.
#   a question nothing proposes     settled `obsolete` by the git io's sweep, every minute, and
#                                   asked again if its tree is proposed again
#
# THIS INSTANCE: the git io reflects the repository as `git.{{.Repo}}` with `-tree` naming the
# charters directory and `-out`, the github io reflects {{.GitHub}} with the same `-tree` and reads
# its out place, and the git io owns `{{.InstallAt}}` or holds `child-owner` above it, since it
# installs as itself.

# ONE PACKAGE PER REPOSITORY, at `repo-charters.<repo>`, so a deployment reconciling two
# repositories installs two packages, and every outbox below is a plain name inside the package:
# a `from` is relative, and one spelled with the repository's id in front (`verse.install`) reads
# as verse's own half when the repository is called `verse`.
at: repo-charters.{{.Repo}}
reads: [git.{{.Repo}}, github.{{.GitHubOrg}}.{{.GitHubName}}, verse.schedule]
# Every minute: verse/live is checked against what is installed, and the questions about trees
# nothing proposes any more are settled `obsolete`.
schedules:
- name: minute
  every: 1m
# The outboxes are places inside the package, which is where the rules below derive the inputs.
sends:
- from: install
  to: git.{{.Repo}}.out
- from: check
  to: git.{{.Repo}}.out
- from: livecheck
  to: git.{{.Repo}}.out
- from: out.git
  to: git.{{.Repo}}.out
- from: out.github
  to: github.{{.GitHubOrg}}.{{.GitHubName}}.out
# The levels, lowest first: what the git io reflects, the pull requests the github io reflects,
# what is on main and on verse/live, the checks and their verdicts, the candidates, what verse
# says about each tree on a pull request, what each pull request proposes now, the minute's ticks,
# the check of verse/live against what is installed, the install the git io is asked for and its
# answer, what runs, and the effects. A derive lands strictly
# above its rule, and a read inside a derive at or below it.
strata:
- name: reflected
  level: 0
  match: {path: git}
- name: pulls
  level: 1
  match: {path: "github.{{.GitHubOrg}}.{{.GitHubName}}.pr"}
- name: state
  level: 2
  match: {path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.state"}
- name: checks
  level: 3
  match: {path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.check"}
- name: candidates
  level: 5
  match: {path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.candidate"}
- name: verdicts
  level: 6
  match: {path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.verdict"}
- name: proposals
  level: 7
  match: {path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.pull"}
- name: ticks
  level: 8
  match: {path: 'verse.schedule."repo-charters.{{.Repo}}:minute".tick'}
- name: livecheck
  level: 9
  match: {path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.livecheck"}
- name: install
  level: 10
  match: {path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.install"}
- name: running
  level: 20
  match: {path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.running"}
- name: out
  level: 30
  match: {path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.out"}
rules:
# What is on main: its sha and the charters tree, a candidate per tree ever seen there, and the
# check of that tree.
- name: main
  stratum: reflected
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "git.{{.Repo}}.ref.main"
      value: {tree: {{"{"}}{{.Tree}}: !glob "*"}}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.state"
      id: main
      value: {sha: $subject.value.sha, tree: $subject.value.tree.{{.Tree}}}
    - path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.candidate"
      id: $subject.value.tree.{{.Tree}}
      value: {sha: $subject.value.sha, tree: $subject.value.tree.{{.Tree}}}
    - path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.check"
      id: $subject.value.tree.{{.Tree}}
      value: {to: "check.{{.InstallAt}}", tree: $subject.value.tree.{{.Tree}}}
# What is on verse/live, which is what runs once installed: its sha and the charters tree.
- name: live
  stratum: reflected
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "git.{{.Repo}}.ref.\"verse/live\""
      value: {tree: {{"{"}}{{.Tree}}: !glob "*"}}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.state"
      id: live
      value: {sha: $subject.value.sha, tree: $subject.value.tree.{{.Tree}}}
# An open pull request to main that changes the charters proposes the tree at its head: a
# candidate and its check. The github io's `-tree` reports `changes` against the head's merge
# base with main, so a pull request that leaves the charters alone proposes nothing, and neither
# does one branched before main's charters last changed; one that removes the directory proposes
# the empty tree. What each open pull request to main proposes NOW is a record of its own,
# `pull.<number>`, with an empty `tree` where it proposes nothing: a pull request moves between
# trees, and what verse says on it follows the tree it proposes, not one it proposed before.
- name: pull
  stratum: pulls
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "github.{{.GitHubOrg}}.{{.GitHubName}}.pr.*"
      value: {state: open, base: main, headSha: !glob "*", changes: {{"{"}}{{.Tree}}: !glob "*"}}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.candidate"
      id: $subject.value.changes.{{.Tree}}
      value: {tree: $subject.value.changes.{{.Tree}}, seen: $subject.value.updatedAt}
    - path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.check"
      id: $subject.value.changes.{{.Tree}}
      value: {to: "check.{{.InstallAt}}", tree: $subject.value.changes.{{.Tree}}}
    - path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.pull"
      id: $subject.value.number
      value: {pr: $subject.value.number, head: $subject.value.headSha, tree: $subject.value.changes.{{.Tree}}}
- name: pull-quiet
  stratum: pulls
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "github.{{.GitHubOrg}}.{{.GitHubName}}.pr.*"
      value: !and [{state: open, base: main, headSha: !glob "*"}, !not.has-path changes.{{.Tree}}]
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.pull"
      id: $subject.value.number
      value: {pr: $subject.value.number, head: $subject.value.headSha, tree: ""}
# THE CHECK, apart from the decision: the git io asks the verse's charter check what an install
# of a tree would do (iod reference, the out half of git), and its verdict is written onto the
# candidate, where the pull requests' `verse/check` statuses are drawn from.
- name: checked
  stratum: checks
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.check.*"
      value: {verdict: installs, tree: !glob "*"}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.candidate"
      id: $subject.value.tree
      value: {checked: installs}
- name: check-refused
  stratum: checks
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.check.*"
      value: {verdict: refused, tree: !glob "*"}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.candidate"
      id: $subject.value.tree
      value: {checked: refused, refusal: $subject.value.refusal}
# ONE STANDING ISSUE says main's charters would be refused, and it is closed when main passes:
# opened (or reopened) when the check of main's tree is refused, saying which tree and why, and
# closed when the check of main's tree finds it installs. One issue rather than one per tree,
# because closing is a write onto the issue's input, and a rule can name one input but not every
# tree that ever failed.
- name: main-refused
  stratum: checks
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.check.*"
      value: !let
        let:
        - mainTree: !get-path {at: [repo-charters, {{.Repo}}, state, main], path: tree}
        in: {verdict: refused, tree: .[mainTree]}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.out.github"
      id: check-refused
      value:
        to: issue
        open:
          title: "charters: main's charters would be refused"
          body: "verse's charter check says an install of the charters on main (tree $subject.value.tree) into {{.InstallAt}} would be refused:\n\n$subject.value.refusal\n\nThis issue closes when the charters on main pass the check."
        closed: false
- name: main-passed
  stratum: checks
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.check.*"
      value: !let
        let:
        - mainTree: !get-path {at: [repo-charters, {{.Repo}}, state, main], path: tree}
        in: {verdict: installs, tree: .[mainTree]}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.out.github"
      id: check-refused
      value: {closed: true}
# EACH CANDIDATE IS NAMED for its question and its pull request's title: a tree is installed,
# and the empty tree, the charters directory with nothing in it or gone, is every charter at
# {{.InstallAt}} removed. Named once, latched on the name.
- name: name-tree
  stratum: candidates
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.candidate.*"
      value: !and [!not.has-path ask, {tree: !not "{{.EmptyTree}}"}]
  action:
    patch:
      ask: "Install the charters tree $subject.value.tree into {{.InstallAt}}?"
      title: "install the charters tree $subject.value.tree"
      seen: "1970-01-01T00:00:00Z"
- name: name-removal
  stratum: candidates
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.candidate.*"
      value: !and [!not.has-path ask, {tree: "{{.EmptyTree}}"}]
  action:
    patch:
      ask: "Remove every charter at {{.InstallAt}}? The repository's charters directory holds none."
      title: "remove every charter at {{.InstallAt}}"
      seen: "1970-01-01T00:00:00Z"
# A candidate nobody has answered is asked about: a task whose one call is the ask, so the
# question is the task's and its answer is the task's result, written onto the candidate. The
# answer stands for the TREE, whichever commit carries it from then on. The question's id is the
# task's with its first call's number, which is how the pull requests below name it.
#
# OBSOLETE IS THE THIRD ANSWER: the minute's sweep has the git io settle a question whose tree
# nothing proposes any more (`obsolete-sweep`), so no question stands for nothing. Such a tree is
# asked about again once something proposes it again: once main holds it, or once a pull request
# proposing it moves on from when it was asked. A pull request writes `seen`, its `updatedAt`, and
# the answer records `askedAt`, the `seen` it was asked at (a candidate is named with a floor).
- name: propose
  stratum: candidates
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.candidate.*"
      value: !let
        let:
        - mainTree: !get-path {at: [repo-charters, {{.Repo}}, state, main], path: tree}
        in: !and
        - {ask: !glob "*", seen: !glob "*"}
        - !or
          - !not.has-path answer
          - {answer: {{.Obsolete}}, tree: .[mainTree]}
          - {answer: {{.Obsolete}}, seen: !gt.get-path(root) askedAt}
  action:
    spawn:
      vector: auto
      calls:
      - tool: ask
        input:
          question: "$ask"
          options: [proceed, deny, obsolete]{{if .Answerers}}
          answerers: {{.AnswerersList}}{{end}}
    onDone:
      patch: {answer: $result, askedAt: $subject.value.seen}
# WHAT VERSE SAYS ABOUT A TREE on any pull request that proposes it, from its answer and its
# check: the decision (the `verse` status), the check (the `verse/check` status, and the comment's
# text, which says why a refused tree would be refused), and the hold (verse's review), which
# needs both.
# A NEW TREE'S VERDICT is written whole, pending on both counts, in one derive: the rule drawing
# a pull request from it reads every field, and one that found half a verdict would fail and
# wait to be tried again. After that each count is its own: the answer rewrites the decision,
# the check the check and the text.
- name: verdict-new
  stratum: candidates
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.candidate.*"
      value: !and [!not.has-path answer, !not.has-path checked]
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.verdict"
      id: $subject.value.tree
      value:
        decision:
          state: pending
          description: "waiting for an answer; merging is held until then"
        check: {state: pending, description: "checking against what runs"}
        review: {event: request-changes, body: "verse: this changes the charters verse runs, and waits for an answer to the question on this pull request."}
        text: "This pull request changes the charters verse runs for this repository. Merging waits for an answer to the question below, and a yes takes effect once this merges."
- name: verdict-yes
  stratum: candidates
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.candidate.*"
      value: {answer: {{.Proceed}}}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.verdict"
      id: $subject.value.tree
      value:
        decision:
          state: success
          description: "answered yes"
- name: verdict-no
  stratum: candidates
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.candidate.*"
      value: {answer: {{.Deny}}}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.verdict"
      id: $subject.value.tree
      value:
        decision:
          state: failure
          description: "answered no"
        # The hold for a no does not wait on the check, so it is written with the decision: the
        # status and the review never disagree in between.
        review: {event: request-changes, body: "verse: answered no ($subject.value.answer). These charters do not take effect."}
- name: verdict-installs
  stratum: candidates
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.candidate.*"
      value: {checked: installs}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.verdict"
      id: $subject.value.tree
      value:
        check: {state: success, description: "installs cleanly over what runs"}
        text: "This pull request changes the charters verse runs for this repository. Merging waits for an answer to the question below, and a yes takes effect once this merges."
- name: verdict-refused
  stratum: candidates
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.candidate.*"
      value: {checked: refused}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.verdict"
      id: $subject.value.tree
      value:
        check: {state: failure, description: "would be refused; see the comment"}
        text: "This pull request changes the charters verse runs for this repository. Merging waits for an answer to the question below, and a yes takes effect once this merges.\n\nverse's charter check says these charters would be refused if installed into {{.InstallAt}}:\n\n$subject.value.refusal"
# THE HOLD, verse's review, lifts only when BOTH halves agree: the question answered yes AND the
# charter check finding the tree installs. A yes on a tree the check has not answered yet, or
# refuses, leaves the merge held, saying what it waits for, so a tree the install would refuse is
# never let through on a yes alone. One rule per state of the two, so no two write it at once; a
# no's hold is written with its decision (`verdict-no`), since it waits on nothing.
- name: hold-unanswered
  stratum: candidates
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.candidate.*"
      value: !or [!not.has-path answer, {answer: {{.Obsolete}}}]
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.verdict"
      id: $subject.value.tree
      value:
        review: {event: request-changes, body: "verse: this changes the charters verse runs, and waits for an answer to the question on this pull request."}
- name: hold-yes-unchecked
  stratum: candidates
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.candidate.*"
      value: !and [{answer: {{.Proceed}}}, !not.has-path checked]
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.verdict"
      id: $subject.value.tree
      value:
        review: {event: request-changes, body: "verse: answered yes; waiting for verse's charter check of these charters before the merge."}
- name: hold-yes-refused
  stratum: candidates
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.candidate.*"
      value: {answer: {{.Proceed}}, checked: refused}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.verdict"
      id: $subject.value.tree
      value:
        review: {event: request-changes, body: "verse: answered yes, but verse's charter check refuses these charters (see the comment); the merge stays held until they pass."}
- name: hold-lifted
  stratum: candidates
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.candidate.*"
      value: {answer: {{.Proceed}}, checked: installs}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.verdict"
      id: $subject.value.tree
      value:
        review: {event: dismiss, body: "verse: answered yes, and the charter check passes; this takes effect once merged."}
# ON THE PULL REQUEST TO MAIN, what verse says about the tree it proposes now: the comment with
# the question, verse's review, and both statuses on its head, drawn from that tree's verdict. Re
# drawn when any verdict changes (`when`), since what is read in an action is copied, not
# followed. One input per pull request, so a pull request that moves to another tree is asked
# about that tree in the same comment, under the same review.
- name: pr-hold
  stratum: proposals
  condition:
    when:
      path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.verdict.*"
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.pull.*"
      value: {tree: !not ""}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.out.github.pr"
      id: $subject.value.pr
      value:
        to: "pr.$subject.value.pr"
        tree: $subject.value.tree
        text: !get-path {at: [repo-charters, {{.Repo}}, verdict, $subject.value.tree], path: text}
        asks: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}:propose@repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.candidate.$subject.value.tree~1"
        review: !get-path {at: [repo-charters, {{.Repo}}, verdict, $subject.value.tree], path: review}
        commitStatus:
          verse:
            state: !get-path {at: [repo-charters, {{.Repo}}, verdict, $subject.value.tree], path: decision.state}
            description: !get-path {at: [repo-charters, {{.Repo}}, verdict, $subject.value.tree], path: decision.description}
            sha: $subject.value.head
          "verse/check":
            state: !get-path {at: [repo-charters, {{.Repo}}, verdict, $subject.value.tree], path: check.state}
            description: !get-path {at: [repo-charters, {{.Repo}}, verdict, $subject.value.tree], path: check.description}
            sha: $subject.value.head
# A pull request that no longer changes the charters is held for nothing: the review dismissed and
# the comment saying so. One that never did writes an input with no `to`, which no io reads.
- name: pr-quiet
  stratum: proposals
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.pull.*"
      value: {tree: ""}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.out.github.pr"
      id: $subject.value.pr
      value:
        tree: ""
        text: "This pull request no longer changes the charters verse runs, so verse asks nothing here."
        asks: []
        review: {event: dismiss, body: "verse: this pull request no longer changes the charters."}
# ON THE verse/live PULL REQUEST, for a change that reached main without an answer: while a
# candidate is asked about, its question rides a pull request from a branch at the candidate's
# commit, opened onto verse/live, so its diff is what is on main against what runs, with the
# question as its comment and the answer heard there, and verse's commit status pending on its
# head. Only once verse/live stands, and only while the candidate is main's and not what
# verse/live holds; one main has moved past is closed as superseded.
- name: candidate-branch
  stratum: candidates
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.candidate.*"
      # The commit is read so that the branch follows main to a new commit of this tree.
      value: !and [!not.has-path answer, {sha: !glob "*"}]
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.out.git"
      id: $subject.value.tree
      value: {to: "branch.verse/candidate/$subject.value.tree", sha: $subject.value.sha}
- name: candidate-pr
  stratum: candidates
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.candidate.*"
      value: !let
        let:
        - mainTree: !get-path {at: [repo-charters, {{.Repo}}, state, main], path: tree}
        - mainSha: !get-path {at: [repo-charters, {{.Repo}}, state, main], path: sha}
        # A read of what does not stand matches nothing, so this rule waits for verse/live.
        - liveTree: !get-path {at: [repo-charters, {{.Repo}}, state, live], path: tree}
        in: !and
        - !not.has-path answer
        # The commit is read so that main moving to a new commit of this tree redraws the commit status.
        - {tree: .[mainTree], sha: .[mainSha], title: !glob "*"}
        - {tree: !not .[liveTree]}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.out.github"
      id: $subject.value.tree
      value:
        to: "branch.verse/candidate/$subject.value.tree"
        open: {base: "verse/live", title: "charters: $subject.value.title"}
        text: "What is on main against what runs. An answer here installs it."
        asks: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}:propose@repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.candidate.$subject.value.tree~1"
        commitStatus: {verse: {state: pending, description: "waiting for an answer to the question on this pull request", sha: $subject.value.sha}}
        closed: false
# The check's verdict on the verse/live pull request of a tree main carries, while it is asked
# about there.
- name: live-checked
  stratum: candidates
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.candidate.*"
      value: !and [!not.has-path answer, {checked: installs, sha: !glob "*"}]
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.out.github"
      id: $subject.value.tree
      value:
        commitStatus: {"verse/check": {state: success, description: "installs cleanly over what runs"}}
- name: live-check-refused
  stratum: candidates
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.candidate.*"
      value: !and [!not.has-path answer, {checked: refused, sha: !glob "*"}]
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.out.github"
      id: $subject.value.tree
      value:
        commitStatus: {"verse/check": {state: failure, description: "would be refused; see the issue"}}
- name: candidate-superseded
  stratum: candidates
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.candidate.*"
      value: !let
        let:
        - mainTree: !get-path {at: [repo-charters, {{.Repo}}, state, main], path: tree}
        in: !and
        - !not.has-path answer
        - {tree: !not .[mainTree], sha: !glob "*"}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.out.github"
      id: $subject.value.tree
      value: {closed: true}
# A no closes the candidate's pull request, with verse's commit status failed on its head: the record of
# the decision, where the question was asked.
- name: candidate-denied
  stratum: candidates
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.candidate.*"
      value: {answer: {{.Deny}}, sha: !glob "*"}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.out.github"
      id: $subject.value.tree
      value:
        closed: true
        commitStatus: {verse: {state: failure, description: "denied: $subject.value.answer", sha: $subject.value.sha}}
# A yes on main's tree makes verse/live hold it: made at the candidate's commit where verse/live
# does not stand yet (the first install), and moved there where it stands at another tree (a
# later yes, or a rollback to a tree answered before). Two inputs for the one branch, because a
# read of verse/live is nothing before it stands, and a rule reading nothing never fires.
- name: live-made
  stratum: candidates
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.candidate.*"
      value: !let
        let:
        - mainTree: !get-path {at: [repo-charters, {{.Repo}}, state, main], path: tree}
        - mainSha: !get-path {at: [repo-charters, {{.Repo}}, state, main], path: sha}
        in: {answer: {{.Proceed}}, tree: .[mainTree], sha: .[mainSha]}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.out.git"
      id: live-made
      value: {to: "branch.verse/live", sha: $subject.value.sha, create: true}
- name: live-moved
  stratum: candidates
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.candidate.*"
      value: !let
        let:
        - mainTree: !get-path {at: [repo-charters, {{.Repo}}, state, main], path: tree}
        - mainSha: !get-path {at: [repo-charters, {{.Repo}}, state, main], path: sha}
        - liveTree: !get-path {at: [repo-charters, {{.Repo}}, state, live], path: tree}
        in: !and
        - {answer: {{.Proceed}}, tree: .[mainTree], sha: .[mainSha]}
        - {tree: !not .[liveTree]}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.out.git"
      id: live
      value: {to: "branch.verse/live", sha: $subject.value.sha}
# The tree verse/live holds is installed, where its candidate was answered yes: the git io is
# asked to install it, at verse/live's commit. The input is named for the tree and carries the
# commit, so the io does nothing for a tree it already installed for that commit, and a commit
# to main moves nothing here: only verse/live moving does.
- name: install
  stratum: candidates
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.candidate.*"
      value: !let
        let:
        - liveTree: !get-path {at: [repo-charters, {{.Repo}}, state, live], path: tree}
        in: {answer: {{.Proceed}}, tree: .[liveTree]}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.install"
      id: $subject.value.tree
      value:
        to: "install.{{.InstallAt}}"
        tree: $subject.value.tree
        sha: !get-path {at: [repo-charters, {{.Repo}}, state, live], path: sha}
# The io's answer: the tree it installed and for which commit. What runs is recorded, the
# candidate's pull request's commit status passes, and the tree is tagged once at the commit it first ran
# from. ONLY THE ANSWER FOR verse/live's COMMIT: a rule fires for every status it selects at every
# commit under its place, and what runs is one record, so the statuses of earlier installs must
# not be selected, or whichever fired last would be what runs.
- name: installed
  stratum: install
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.install.*"
      value: !let
        let:
        - liveSha: !get-path {at: [repo-charters, {{.Repo}}, state, live], path: sha}
        in: !and
        - {address: !glob "*", tree: !glob "*", sha: .[liveSha]}
        - !not.has-path error
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}"
      id: running
      value: {tree: $subject.value.tree, sha: $subject.value.sha}
    # Merged at depth onto the candidate's pull request input, where one was opened; where none
    # was (the first install, a rollback), this stands alone with no to, and no io reads it.
    - path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.out.github"
      id: $subject.value.tree
      value:
        commitStatus: {verse: {state: success, description: "installed at $subject.value.sha", sha: $subject.value.sha}}
    # A refused install's issue is closed by an install that succeeds.
    - path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.out.github"
      id: install-failed
      value: {closed: true}
    # The tag names the TREE and stands at the commit it first ran from, once: a later commit
    # carrying the same tree finds the tag standing and leaves it.
    - path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.out.git"
      key: $subject.value.tree
      value: {to: "tag.live-verse/$subject.value.tree", sha: $subject.value.sha, create: true}
# An install the io refused for good is an issue in the repository, saying what the verse said,
# and the candidate's pull request's commit status fails. The issue and the status stand until the
# input changes: a fix on main is a new tree, a new candidate and a new question.
- name: install-failed
  stratum: install
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.install.*"
      value: {failed: permanent, tree: !glob "*"}
  action:
    derive:
    # ONE STANDING ISSUE for a refused install, as for a refused check: opened (or reopened)
    # here, saying which tree and commit and why, and closed by the next install that succeeds.
    - path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.out.github"
      id: install-failed
      value:
        to: issue
        open:
          title: "charters: the install of what verse/live holds failed"
          body: "verse refused to install the charters tree $subject.value.tree at commit $subject.value.sha into {{.InstallAt}}:\n\n$subject.value.error\n\nThis issue closes when an install succeeds."
        closed: false
    - path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.out.github"
      id: $subject.value.tree
      value:
        commitStatus: {verse: {state: failure, description: "the install failed; see the issue", sha: $subject.value.sha}}
# MAIN'S CHARTERS ANSWERED NO: what runs stays as it was, and an issue says so while main holds the
# denied tree. It closes when main's charters are anything else.
- name: main-denied
  stratum: candidates
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.candidate.*"
      value: !let
        let:
        - mainTree: !get-path {at: [repo-charters, {{.Repo}}, state, main], path: tree}
        in: {tree: .[mainTree], answer: {{.Deny}}}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.out.github"
      id: main-denied
      value:
        to: issue
        open:
          title: "charters: main's charters were answered no"
          body: "The charters on main (tree $subject.value.tree) were answered no: $subject.value.answer.\n\nWhat runs at {{.InstallAt}} stays as it was. This issue closes when main's charters change."
        closed: false
- name: main-not-denied
  stratum: candidates
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.candidate.*"
      value: !let
        let:
        - mainTree: !get-path {at: [repo-charters, {{.Repo}}, state, main], path: tree}
        in: !and [{tree: .[mainTree]}, !not {answer: {{.Deny}}}]
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.out.github"
      id: main-denied
      value: {closed: true}
# EVERY MINUTE, verse/live against what is installed: the git io resolves verse/live in its own
# copy and asks the verse's charter check what installing its tree would change (iod reference,
# the check of a ref). Only once verse/live has stood: before the first install there is nothing
# to compare.
- name: live-check
  stratum: ticks
  condition:
    subject:
      path: 'verse.schedule."repo-charters.{{.Repo}}:minute".tick.*'
      value: !let
        let:
        - liveSha: !get-path {at: [repo-charters, {{.Repo}}, state, live], path: sha}
        in: {due: !glob "*"}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.livecheck"
      id: live
      value: {to: "check.{{.InstallAt}}", ref: "verse/live", of: {{.Tree}}, round: $subject.value.due}
# verse/live HOLDING SOMETHING OTHER THAN WHAT RUNS is an open issue: neither restored nor asked
# about at the time. The ask comes when somebody opens a pull request to fix it; moving verse/live
# back in step closes it. Two rounds in a row at one commit, so an install verse is making is not
# taken for it.
- name: live-in-step
  stratum: livecheck
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.livecheck.*"
      value: {ref: "verse/live", verdict: installs, changes: 0}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.out.github"
      id: live-drift
      value: {closed: true}
- name: live-drifted
  stratum: livecheck
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.livecheck.*"
      value: {ref: "verse/live", verdict: installs, changes: !gt 0, differingFor: !ge 2}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.out.github"
      id: live-drift
      value:
        to: issue
        open:
          title: "charters: verse/live is not what runs"
          body: "verse/live (commit $subject.value.sha, tree $subject.value.tree) differs from the charters installed at {{.InstallAt}} by $subject.value.changes charter(s). Nothing installs from it while it does.\n\nOpen a pull request to main to change the charters, or move verse/live back in step; this issue closes when it is."
        closed: false
- name: live-refused
  stratum: livecheck
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.livecheck.*"
      value: {ref: "verse/live", verdict: refused, differingFor: !ge 2}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.out.github"
      id: live-drift
      value:
        to: issue
        open:
          title: "charters: verse/live is not what runs"
          body: "verse/live (commit $subject.value.sha, tree $subject.value.tree) holds charters an install at {{.InstallAt}} would refuse:\n\n$subject.value.refusal\n\nOpen a pull request to main to change the charters, or move verse/live back in step; this issue closes when it is."
        closed: false
- name: live-missing
  stratum: livecheck
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.livecheck.*"
      value: {ref: "verse/live", verdict: missing}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.out.github"
      id: live-drift
      value:
        to: issue
        open:
          title: "charters: verse/live is not what runs"
          body: "verse/live is gone from the repository, so nothing records what runs at {{.InstallAt}} there.\n\nPut verse/live back at the commit verse put it at; this issue closes when it is."
        closed: false
# EVERY MINUTE, the questions about trees nothing proposes now are settled `obsolete` by the git io:
# what is proposed is main's tree, verse/live's, and each open pull request to main's change, read
# at the tick, and only questions posed by the tick are settled.
- name: obsolete-sweep
  stratum: ticks
  condition:
    subject:
      path: 'verse.schedule."repo-charters.{{.Repo}}:minute".tick.*'
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.out.git"
      id: obsolete
      value:
        to: obsolete
        prefix: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}:propose@repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.candidate."
        before: $subject.value.due
        keep:
          state: !list-paths {at: "repo-charters.{{.Repo}}.state.*", path: tree}
          pulls: !list-paths {at: "github.{{.GitHubOrg}}.{{.GitHubName}}.pr.*", value: {state: open, base: main}, path: changes.{{.Tree}}}
